**Describe the performance issue**
JavaScript Couch views are [known](https://docs.couchdb.org/en/stable/maintenance/performance.html#views-generation) to have terrible performance when there is a large number of docs:

> Views with the JavaScript query server are extremely slow to generate when there are a non-trivial number of documents to process. . . .  The cause is the latency involved in the CouchDB server and separate couchjs query server

**Describe the improvement you'd like**
As a part of https://github.com/medic/cht-core/issues/8462, I [investigated](https://medic.slack.com/archives/C02FMC810UE/p1706672125231989) the performance differences between using a JS view to retrieve the user-device data for 1000 users (from a db containing 200,000 docs in total) vs using an [Erlang view](https://docs.couchdb.org/en/stable/config/query-servers.html#query-servers-definition) to do the same thing. Local testing showed a `+10x` improvement in response time from the Erlang view (`3sec`) vs the JS view (`36sec`). 

Of course, Erlang views will not be usable in Pouch, so this is not relevant for any views used within the webapp.  However, it seems reasonable to conclude that views used purely on the server-side (or in the admin app) could experience a pretty significant performance bump by being converted to Erlang.  

**Describe alternatives you've considered**
For the sake of completeness, when I was testing the JS view performance, I did check to see if setting `sorted=false` or `lazy=false` had any meaningful impact on the response time.  In my tests, it did not have any measurable affect.

**To Reproduce**

